Lobium - native WebRTC policy semantics.

The launcher applies Chromium's --webrtc-ip-handling-policy as defense in depth (NOT the "force"
variant, which only content/shell and headless/lib read - a chrome/ build ignores it entirely, so
that flag was doing nothing at all until 2026-08-22). This hook makes the product policies real in
Blink:
  * default_public_interface_only: Chromium default-public-interface policy.
  * disable_non_proxied_udp: Chromium blocks UDP paths that bypass the configured proxy; Lobium also
    redacts the local endpoint from permitted TURN TCP/TLS failure events.
  * proxy_only: forces relay-only ICE, suppresses non-relay candidates, and redacts the local endpoint
    from relay failure events while keeping their URL/error details.
  * disabled: the connection constructs normally and never surfaces a candidate or candidate error.

The sidecar independently rejects proxy_only without a configured proxy before browser spawn.

THREE THINGS THIS PATCH USED TO GET WRONG, all page-observable:

1. "disabled" threw NotSupportedError from RTCPeerConnection::Create with the message
   "WebRTC is disabled by this browser profile." That message is a product signature readable by one
   try/catch, and rewording it does not help: real Chrome never throws from that constructor in an
   attached document, so the throw ITSELF is the tell. The policy is now enforced in
   DidGenerateICECandidate, where "no candidates" is indistinguishable from a network that produces
   none - and with no candidates there is no connectivity, so the policy still means what it says.

2. proxy_only assigned configuration.type = kRelay, and getConfiguration() reads that back through
   peer_handler_->GetConfiguration(). A page constructing with iceTransportPolicy:'all' therefore
   read back 'relay' - the browser contradicting the page's own dictionary, which no Chrome does.
   The forced value is still applied to the live configuration, because suppressing candidates later
   is not equivalent: they also reach script through localDescription.sdp, which a candidate-level
   filter never sees. Instead the page's last successfully requested values are remembered and
   reported by getConfiguration(). The same policy transform is applied atomically in both Create()
   and setConfiguration(): a page cannot restore host/srflx gathering by reconfiguring after the
   constructor, and a rejected reconfiguration cannot corrupt the values getConfiguration() echoes.

3. icecandidateerror is independent from the successful-candidate callback. WebRTC builds TURN
   allocation errors from the raw local socket address and port; Blink copies those values into both
   event.address and event.hostCandidate. A failing page-supplied TURN URL therefore exposed the host
   endpoint even while proxy_only surfaced no host/srflx/prflx candidate. proxy_only and
   disable_non_proxied_udp now preserve useful relay failure signaling (URL, errorCode and errorText)
   but report a null address and port with an empty hostCandidate. disabled suppresses the error event
   as well as candidate events.

MEASURED, not assumed. A first attempt enforced "disabled" purely in DidGenerateICECandidate, and
that leaked: the icecandidate event was silent but pc.localDescription.sdp still carried all four
candidates, host and srflx alike. "disabled" therefore also forces relay-only AND clears
configuration.servers, so nothing can be gathered even when the PAGE supplies its own TURN server -
with the original server list echoed back by getConfiguration() for the same reason as the policy.
Verified on the built engine:

    control     constructs=true  iceTransportPolicy=all  candidates=4 {host:2,srflx:2}  sdpLines=4
    proxy_only  constructs=true  iceTransportPolicy=all  candidates=0                   sdpLines=0
    disabled    constructs=true  iceTransportPolicy=all  candidates=0                   sdpLines=0

